  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1235 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nRain continues to fall during these early morning hours, though we \nare seeing some breaks in the banded rainfall structure. Concern for \nthe near term is whether any snowflakes will mix in. We've seen a \nfew \"unknown precip\" or snow observations across north central \nIndiana with cold advection creeping up from the south, not a common \ndirection for that. Dewpoints across these areas are largely in the \nmid 30s, save an isolated 34 in spots. With these readings, \nexpect no impacts. Vorticity maximum will continue to migrate \nthough the area this morning before height rises commence in \nconjunction with the loss of deeper moisture. As the deep storm \nsystem pulls away from the region, expect slowly thinning clouds\nand winds easing later in the day. Warm advection from the \nnorthwest and the loss of the lower cloud cover will allow \ntemperatures to recover to the mid 40s in Illinois, though our \nIndiana locales will only make it to the low 40s.\n\nThe meridional upper pattern over the area due the deep storm system \nout east will get replaced with a more progressive pattern, \nevidenced by a strong upper jet and a bit more zonal flow across\nthe northwest United States. The upper jet will drive a series \nof waves toward the area, both tonight and then on Sunday. \n\nWe may actually scatter out the clouds this evening ahead of \ntonight's shortwave. This wave looks like it has enough push to \ndrive some precipitation upstream in IA and NW IL, but it may be \nsomewhat moisture starved and the forcing is also limited to a \nshort window late tonight, mainly during the early morning \nhours. Forecast soundings depict a deep enough warm layer to \nkeep any falling precipitation in the liquid form, thus we have \nheld onto a mention for few sprinkles overnight. Saturday is \nshaping up to be a decent fall day with clearing skies and \ntemperatures knocking at the 50 degree door. \n\nA second more potent upper jet will lead a more amplified wave later \nSaturday night into Sunday. Precipitation will come in as rain.\nThe surface reflection will take a favorable track to allow the\ncolder reservoir air of the system to shift into the lower \nGreat Lakes mainly Sunday evening, but not likely in time before\nthe deeper moisture exits to the east. \n\nThe polar jet will quickly lift back while the subtropical jet \nbuilds back into the southwestern United States in El Nino fashion. \nModel guidance is in fair agreement on the overall pattern of a \nstorm system spreading into the SW states early in the week before \nejecting eastward. Ahead of the system, southwest flow will pump in \nseasonally warm airmass as we enter into meteorological winter, \nconsistent with the well above average temperature CPC forecast. \nOutside of any subtle waves ahead of this system, the first half of \nthe week will be dry. Attention will then shift to the latter half \nof the week. With no deep cold air, rain and wind would be the most \nnotable impacts. \n\nKMD",
